    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new step slots into the pipeline well and the model/mapper/schema/OpenAPI additions all mirror the established `bulkSignupWarning`/`membershipLevel` precedent \u2014 the overall shape is idiomatic. One structural consistency issue is worth fixing:\n\n**`FlagPossibleDuplicate` bypasses the codebase's centralized telephone-comparison rule.**\n- This codebase has deliberately funneled all telephone comparison through `OwnerTelephones.toE164` and all duplicate detection \"is now expressed through\" `OwnerIdentity` (see the class Javadoc on both: the helpers exist specifically so the comparisons \"can never drift apart\"). `RejectDuplicateIdentity` \u2014 the hard-duplicate sibling this step explicitly pairs with \u2014 routes every telephone comparison through that helper, precisely so a stored value that predates the pipeline still compares correctly.\n- `FlagPossibleDuplicate` instead reimplements its own raw `telephone.equals(existing.getTelephone())` comparison inline. This is the same soft-duplicate-detection responsibility, so a maintainer would expect it to go through the shared helper like the rest of the duplicate logic. As written, a future change to telephone-normalization semantics (in `OwnerTelephones`) would silently miss this new comparison, which is exactly the drift the centralization was built to prevent.\n- Suggested change: compare telephones via `OwnerTelephones.toE164(...)` (the helper is package-private and in the same package, so it's directly reusable) rather than a bespoke `String.equals`, keeping this step consistent with `RejectDuplicateIdentity`/`OwnerIdentity`.\n\nMinor (not blocking on its own): the manual `matchId` min-tracking loop is fine and matches the `for`-loop style in `RejectDuplicateIdentity`, so no change needed there.",
